  "final_span": "some hard results this season and are out of the Champions League so their home form is under more scrutiny.\n\"We are focused on progressing. I think the boys have been frustrated at our level because results are good but there are things we can be doing better. We are trying to put things together.\"\nThere are some minor worries, especially in attack, as prolific center-forward Robin van Persie recovers from a muscle injury resulting from a challenging tackle. There remains uncertainty about how manager David Moyes will set his lineup before the match, and whether Rooney's absence offers van Persie a chance to appear for a few minutes with his right foot, rather than substituting him after an hour, which is currently the planned strategy for Sunday.\nThere is little doubt this time, as it is certain to be much better.\n\"Some good things are in there, but there is still a lot of improvement,\" said Moyes. \"We are not blind either.\n\"We have been getting a lot of good chances in some games and let poor scoring decisions stand in the way. I don't talk too much publicly about opposition teams and methods but all I've had from them for the game on Tuesday is they are very much settled and everything they are trying to do is very well, they've handled the squad well and gotten something out of them - we are lucky that is the case.\n\"They are very well organised and know what they are trying to do. We know we are as good a football team now as any in England plus we've added the two Dutch players.\n\"It would be great if we got through this tough period and into some better form. We tend to find our way at the business end of the season when other teams get weary.\n\"We must keep focused, and sometimes we have drawn sand in a few games, but soon will get points on the board.\"\nShaky and solid early-season City performers recently ventured back to the heart of Leeds' historical and physical center and deployed time-honored skill to secure three points with a 1-0 win.\nThey will now face Burnley in an away test match for the third time during the campaign, marking Dyche's first encounter at Old Trafford.\n\"Mike and he's",
